INT. ROLLER RINK - AFTER HOURS - NIGHT

Disco ball off. Work lights on. Scuffed maple floor. A snack bar with a hand-lettered menu.

Chairs on tables. Mop bucket. Silence.

JUNE PAIK (16, denim jacket, fresh bruise on one knee) laces up skates at the rail. Alone. Breathing.

She stands. Wobbles. Pushes off.

Slow loops. Clumsy. Then smoother.

On the far wall: a trophy case. One photo. A girl in a gold jumpsuit, mid-spin.

June stares at it as she passes.

A buzz. The wall phone behind the snack bar rings.

June ignores it. Another lap.

It keeps ringing.

She skates to the counter. Picks up.

JUNE
Rink's closed.

MRS. PAIK (V.O.)
(over phone)
June. Where are you?

JUNE
Studying. At Hana's.

MRS. PAIK (V.O.)
Hana's mother says you left at five.

JUNE
I went to the library after.

MRS. PAIK (V.O.)
The library closes at six.

June closes her eyes.

JUNE
I'm fine, Mom.

MRS. PAIK (V.O.)
Come home.

JUNE
In an hour.

She hangs up. Turns.

A man in the doorway. DELROY BASS (60s, rink owner, janitor's keys on his belt, shoulders that sag a little to the left).

DELROY
Thought I heard someone.

JUNE
Sorry. The back door was open.

DELROY
Back door's never open.

JUNE
It was today.

He looks at her skates. At the bruise.

DELROY
That your mother?

JUNE
Yeah.

DELROY
She sounds worried.

JUNE
She's always worried.

Delroy nods. Hangs his keys on a nail.

DELROY
You skate like her.

JUNE
Like who?

He points at the trophy case.

DELROY
Valerie Paik. Nineteen eighty-four. Regional champ.

June stares at him.

JUNE
That's my mother?

DELROY
She never told you.

JUNE
She told me she hated skating.

DELROY
She did. After she quit.

JUNE
Why?

Delroy takes a long breath.

DELROY
Ask her.

June sits on the rail. Skates dangling. She looks at the photo. The girl in the gold jumpsuit looks back.

Her phone buzzes. A text from Mom: "I'm outside."

She looks up at Delroy.

JUNE
She's here.

DELROY
Then you'd better go.

June stands. Skates to the door. Slows. Turns back.

JUNE
Can I come back tomorrow?

DELROY
Rink opens at ten.

JUNE
Not for open skate.

DELROY
(small smile)
Then at six. I'll leave the back door open.
